00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:07.010
In this video, I’ll teach you when
and how to use local variables.

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:11.860
Local variables are temporary variables
that can only be accessed within

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:13.440
the function that defines them.

00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:17.739
They are useful when you only need
values within a particular expression.

00:00:18.959 --> 00:00:23.120
This is different from rule inputs, which
allow you to pass data to other objects.

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:27.959
Local variables have many uses
in interfaces and expressions.

00:00:28.869 --> 00:00:32.680
You can use a local variable to store
a user input that doesn’t need to be

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:34.680
stored elsewhere, like a search term.

00:00:35.139 --> 00:00:38.940
Or, you may run into them if you
are reusing a calculated value in

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:40.779
multiple places in an expression.

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:45.339
Local variables are also perfect
for holding a temporary value until

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:49.060
you can save it to a rule input to
use elsewhere in your application.

00:00:49.759 --> 00:00:54.480
Let’s look at this short expression that
defines two local variables – one for

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:56.550
the first name and one for the last name.

00:00:56.870 --> 00:01:00.590
You may see an expression like this
one in any simple registration form.

00:01:00.910 --> 00:01:04.899
To get started, you will first need
the function, a!LocalVariables.

00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:09.189
This function allows you to define
as many local variables as you

00:01:09.190 --> 00:01:10.969
need to use within an expression.

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:14.719
All local variables must
use the prefix: local.

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:17.850
Following the prefix is
the name of the variable.

00:01:18.190 --> 00:01:20.670
It is best to keep the
names short and descriptive.

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:24.800
Once you add the values and your
variables are defined, you can

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:26.210
then use them in your expressions.

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:31.740
In this case, the output is the first
and last name combined, Jane Smith.

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:35.840
Because local variables can only be
used within the expression they’re

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:40.740
defined in, you should generally have
a!LocalVariables at the top so that

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:44.220
you can access and use it throughout
your expression or interface.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.980
A local variable’s type is
determined by the value of the

00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:52.550
variable, unlike rule inputs which
have explicitly defined types.

00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:58.070
In this example, both local!firstName
and local!lastName are of type text.

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:01.860
Local variables are not limited
to primitive data types.

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:08.080
You can also use other types of variables
or rules like rule inputs, expression

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:12.310
rules, or process variables to define
the value of your local variables.

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:17.240
Here, the variable, vehicle, is defined
using an expression rule that takes

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:19.289
in a vehicle ID as a rule input.

00:02:19.809 --> 00:02:23.509
When you enter a valid vehicle
ID, you get an output of the

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:25.580
associated vehicle make and model.

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:30.549
Local variables can also be defined
without an initial value at all.

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:34.700
Often, if your variable is used to
store a temporary input, it will be

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:36.649
defined without a value at first.

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:41.429
In this example, the local variable,
answer, does not have a default value.

00:02:42.369 --> 00:02:46.380
However, once the user selects
either true or false, the variable

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:48.180
is assigned the corresponding value.

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:52.870
If you are using local variables
in an interface, you may also need

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:54.859
to consider their refresh behavior.

00:02:55.929 --> 00:03:00.840
By default, all variables defined within
the local variables function refresh

00:03:00.869 --> 00:03:02.960
when any referenced variables change.

00:03:03.490 --> 00:03:08.390
In this vehicle rental form, if the
value of local!borrowDate changes, then

00:03:08.390 --> 00:03:12.829
local!returnDate will automatically
update by 7 days at the same time.

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:16.360
You can change this default
refresh behavior using the

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:18.389
!refreshVariable function.

00:03:19.369 --> 00:03:22.360
We’ll learn more about refresh
behavior in another video.

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:24.899
Now, let’s recap what
you’ve learned so far.

00:03:26.139 --> 00:03:30.150
Local variables define and store
temporary values within an expression.

00:03:30.590 --> 00:03:35.369
To define your local variables, you will
need to use the function a!localVariables.

00:03:36.389 --> 00:03:39.450
They can be defined with or
without an initial value.

00:03:40.250 --> 00:03:43.690
These values refresh when any
referenced variables change.

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:48.739
You can use the refresh variable
function to modify this default behavior.

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:53.679
To learn more about this function, visit
Appian Documentation or check out the

00:03:53.690 --> 00:03:55.890
Interface courses at Academy Online.

00:03:56.320 --> 00:04:04.180
Thank you for watching, and
I’ll see you in the next one!

